# Line endings are part of the sha256 of this repository's evidence trail.
#
# Three release gates compare a file's sha256 against a number recorded in a
# committed *_meta.json and refuse to run when they disagree:
#
#   data/bookings_synth.csv        -> bookings_meta.json      (Model #1, S.3)
#   data/sentiment/domain_test_200.csv -> domain_test_meta.json (Model #2, S.4)
#   data/assistant/assistant_test.csv  -> assistant_test_meta.json (Model #4, S.6)
#
# This machine has core.autocrlf=true, so without this file a fresh clone gets
# CRLF copies of those CSVs, every recorded sha256 stops matching, and each gate
# fails on a machine where nothing is actually wrong. That failure is the good
# case; the bad case is somebody "fixing" it by re-recording the hash and
# retiring a working provenance check.
#
# So: normalise text to LF in the repository AND in the working tree. text=auto
# leaves anything git detects as binary alone; the explicit rules below cover the
# families where a wrong guess would be expensive either way.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Data and evidence. eol=lf is not a preference here, it is the hash contract.
*.csv    text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.jsonl  text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf

# Code and config.
*.py     text eol=lf
*.dart   text eol=lf
*.js     text eol=lf
*.ts     text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.sql    text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts that other tools read; keep their native endings.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.ps1    text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.ico    binary
*.joblib binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.jar    binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks    binary
